  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Luthersville was named after Martin Luther, the 16‑century German reformer, though the town was not founded by Lutherans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://luthersville.gov/about-luthersville"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Georgia General Assembly incorporated Luthersville as a town in 1872, marking its official start as a municipality.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luthersville%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The city sits on a ridge that divides the Chattahoochee River watershed to the west from the Flint River watershed to the east.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luthersville%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Central of Georgia Railroad reached Luthersville at the turn of the 20th century, sparking early economic growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.meriwethercountyga.gov/239/Luthersville-Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Known as the Gateway to Meriwether County, Luthersville is the primary entry point from more populated areas to the north.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://luthersville.gov/about-luthersville"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "From the mid‑1800s until incorporation, the town was called Keith’s Crossroads, and its central intersection remains the busiest in the county.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
